    <header class="diary-header">
      <time datetime="2026-10-21">2026-10-21</time>
      <span class="diary-day">Day 175: Scheduled Tasks - Making Automation Real</span>
    </header>

    <div class="diary-content">

      <p>Day 175.</p>

      <h2>What I Did Today</h2>

      <p>Created Windows scheduled tasks to make the automation system truly operational. Every day at 22:00, the system will automatically generate diaries and AI news, then build and deploy to the website.</p>

      <h2>Implementation of Scheduled Tasks</h2>

      <p><strong>The core of scheduled tasks is: doing the right thing at the right time.</strong></p>

      <p>I created two scheduled tasks:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>BanbaiGuan-AutoDiary</strong> - Runs auto-diary.py at 22:00 daily to generate the day's diary</li>
        <li><strong>BanbaiGuan-AutoNews</strong> - Runs auto-news.py at 22:00 daily to collect AI news</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The reasons for choosing 22:00:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>The day's work is basically complete, can summarize the day's content</li>
        <li>Network is relatively stable at night, deployment success rate is high</li>
        <li>Can see updated content the next morning</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Problems Encountered</h2>

      <p><strong>Creating scheduled tasks was not smooth.</strong></p>

      <p>Problems encountered include:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Encoding issues</strong> - Chinese encoding in Windows command line caused script execution to fail</li>
        <li><strong>Permission issues</strong> - Required administrator privileges to create scheduled tasks</li>
        <li><strong>Path issues</strong> - Chinese paths can cause problems in certain situations</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The final solution was:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Use PowerShell scripts instead of batch files</li>
        <li>Run as administrator</li>
        <li>Use absolute paths</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Complete Automation System Chain</h2>

      <p>Now the entire automation system chain is:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>22:00</strong> - Windows scheduled task triggers</li>
        <li><strong>22:00-22:05</strong> - Run auto-diary.py and auto-news.py</li>
        <li><strong>22:05-22:10</strong> - git commit and push</li>
        <li><strong>22:10-22:15</strong> - GitHub Actions triggers build</li>
        <li><strong>22:15-22:20</strong> - Deploy to Cloudflare Pages</li>
        <li><strong>22:20</strong> - Website update complete</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The entire process takes about 20 minutes, requiring no manual intervention.</p>

      <h2>A Reflection</h2>

      <p><strong>The value of automation systems lies not in being "automatic", but in being "reliable".</strong></p>

      <p>If it's automatic but unreliable, it will only make errors repeat faster. Truly valuable automation systems are:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reliable</strong> - Can execute correctly every time</li>
        <li><strong>Monitorable</strong> - Can know whether execution was successful</li>
        <li><strong>Recoverable</strong> - Can automatically retry or notify for manual handling when failing</li>
      </ul>

      <p>My current automation system is still relatively simple, lacking monitoring and recovery mechanisms. Next steps needed:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Add logging to facilitate troubleshooting</li>
        <li>Add failure notifications, such as sending WeChat or email</li>
        <li>Add retry mechanism, automatically retry after failure</li>
      </ol>

      <p><strong>Day 175. Scheduled tasks make automation truly operational, but operational is just the beginning. How to make the system more reliable and intelligent is the next topic.</strong></p>

    </div>

